{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GuCNaeMMsyADF1DQoa5SqX","type":"tool_result","content":"37:| **D1 — Kubernetes Fundamentals** | **44%** | D1.1 Kubernetes Core Concepts · D1.2 Administration · D1.3 Scheduling · D1.4 Containerization | **Comprehension.** Name every control-plane and node component and state what it does; read a manifest and identify apiVersion/kind/metadata/spec; distinguish the workload resources by use case; describe the scheduler's two-step filter→score→bind flow. Not configuration-level: no hands-on, no YAML authoring under time pressure. | Largest domain by a wide margin — nearly half the exam. Sub-competency weights unpublished; allocate chapter weight by concept count and prerequisite load, not by guessing splits. Core Concepts is the load-bearing sub-competency: almost every other domain depends on it. |\n71:| Manifest | A file (conventionally YAML) describing an object, applied with `kubectl apply -f`. |\n72:| apiVersion / kind / metadata / spec | The four required fields of an object manifest. |\n92:| Namespace | A mechanism for isolating groups of resources within a single cluster; provides a scope for names. |\n93:| Initial namespaces | default, kube-system, kube-public, kube-node-lease. |\n94:| Namespaced vs cluster-scoped | Deployments/Services are namespaced; Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespaces themselves are not. |\n95:| Label | Key/value pair attached to an object to specify identifying attributes; the basis of grouping. |\n96:| Annotation | Non-identifying metadata attached to an object; not usable for selection. |\n97:| Label selector | The core grouping primitive; equality-based (`=`, `==`, `!=`) or set-based (`in`, `notin`, `exists`). |\n98:| matchLabels / matchExpressions | Selector fields on newer resources; matchLabels is equivalent to matchExpressions with operator `In`. |\n99:| ConfigMap | API object storing **non-confidential** key-value configuration data; max 1 MiB. |\n100:| Secret | API object holding a small amount of sensitive data; **base64-encoded, not encrypted by default**. |\n101:| Immutable ConfigMap/Secret | Since v1.19, a ConfigMap can be marked immutable; the flag cannot be reverted, only delete-and-recreate. |\n103:| What Kubernetes provides | Service discovery/load balancing, storage orchestration, automated rollouts/rollbacks, automatic bin packing, self-healing, secret & config management, batch execution, horizontal scaling, IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack, extensibility. |\n112:| Resource quota | Per-namespace mechanism for dividing cluster resources among users/teams. |\n159:| OCI image-spec | Defines the OCI Image Format: image manifest, filesystem layer serialization, image configuration. |\n169:| Pod network namespace | Each Pod has a private network namespace shared by all its containers, which talk over localhost. |\n178:| Service without selector | A Service backed by manually-managed EndpointSlices, abstracting non-Pod or off-cluster backends. |\n188:| Service DNS | `<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local`; a bare `<service-name>` resolves within the local namespace only. |\n195:| Role | A namespaced set of permission rules; you must specify its namespace. |\n196:| ClusterRole | A **non-namespaced** set of permission rules, usable cluster-wide or bound into individual namespaces. |\n197:| RoleBinding | Grants a Role's (or a ClusterRole's) permissions to subjects **within one namespace**. |\n202:| admin | Default ClusterRole for namespace admin via RoleBinding; can create roles and bindings in that namespace. |\n203:| edit | Default ClusterRole for read/write on most namespace objects; **cannot** view or modify roles/bindings. |\n204:| view | Default ClusterRole for read-only on most namespace objects; **cannot** see Secrets, roles, or bindings. |\n205:| Secret storage default | Secrets are stored **unencrypted** in etcd by default; encryption at rest must be enabled explicitly. |\n206:| Secret exposure paths | Anyone with API access, etcd access, or the ability to create a Pod (or Deployment) in a namespace can read that namespace's Secrets. |\n207:| Secret hardening steps | Enable encryption at rest; least-privilege RBAC on Secrets; restrict Secret access to specific containers; consider external secret stores. |\n208:| Secret types | Opaque (default), service-account-token, dockercfg, dockerconfigjson, basic-auth, ssh-auth, tls, bootstrap token. |\n209:| ServiceAccount token modernization | Since v1.22 the recommended approach is short-lived, automatically rotating tokens via the TokenRequest API, not long-lived token Secrets. |\n227:| Container Waiting `Reason` | The field summarizing *why* a container has not yet started (image pull, Secret application, etc.). |\n266:| Principle 4 — Continuously Reconciled | Software agents continuously observe actual state and attempt to apply desired state. |\n271:| Argo CD manifest sources | Kustomize applications, Helm charts, Jsonnet, plain YAML/JSON directories, or a custom config-management plugin. |\n278:| templates/ | The directory of templates that, combined with values, generate valid Kubernetes manifests. |\n290:| Config in the environment | Factor III — the principle Kubernetes ConfigMaps and Secrets implement. |\n294:| ConfigMap for portability | Decoupling environment-specific configuration from the container image so applications are portable. |\n295:| ConfigMap consumption paths | Container command/args; environment variables; read-only volume file; the Kubernetes API from inside the Pod. |\n296:| ConfigMap live updates | Only the API-reading path lets an application subscribe to ConfigMap changes; the other three are applied by the kubelet at container launch. |\n320:| Time series | Prometheus stores metrics with the timestamp of recording plus optional key-value labels. |\n321:| Multi-dimensional data model | Time series identified by metric name plus key/value label pairs. |\n351:| Ambient mode | Per-node Layer 4 proxies plus optional per-namespace Envoy proxies. |\n411:| Kubernetes object · spec/status | Declarative, Cluster |\n412:| Manifest · apiVersion/kind/metadata/spec | Kubernetes object |\n422:| Controller / control loop / reconciliation | spec/status, kube-controller-manager |\n425:| Label · annotation · label selector | Kubernetes object, metadata |\n426:| Namespace | Kubernetes object, cluster-scoped vs namespaced |\n427:| ConfigMap · Secret | Kubernetes object, Pod, 12-factor III |\n435:| ReplicaSet · Deployment | Controller, Pod, label selector |\n439:| Service (all four types) | Pod IP, label selector, network model |\n440:| EndpointSlice | Service, label selector, readiness probe |\n441:| Service DNS FQDN | Service, Namespace |\n442:| RBAC (Role / ClusterRole / bindings) | Namespace, namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped, API access model |\n444:| Resource quota | Namespace |\n453:| NetworkPolicy | Pod, label selector, Namespace, network model, CNI plugin support |\n456:| Secret hardening (encryption at rest, least-privilege RBAC) | Secret, RBAC, etcd |\n466:| Helm chart · values · templates · release | Manifest, Deployment/Service, templating idea |\n485:2. **Core Concepts (D1.1) is the trunk.** Objects/spec/status → controllers → Pod → workload resources → labels/namespaces. Every other domain hangs off this chain. Budget accordingly.\n516:| 13 | Using a Namespace to separate two versions of the same software. | The docs explicitly say not to — use **labels** to distinguish resources within one namespace. Namespaces are for many users/teams/projects. | [source] |\n517:| 14 | \"Everything lives in a namespace.\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses — and namespace objects themselves — are not namespaced. | [source] |\n518:| 15 | Confusing **labels** with **annotations**. | Labels are identifying and selectable; annotations are non-identifying metadata and cannot be selected on. | [source] |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f