{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M7uKXWbJnyqYG5QF2SieVP","type":"tool_result","content":"1315\t## The Voyage Ahead\n1316\t\n1317\tYou've met the crew and you know how they coordinate. What you don't yet have is the thing they're all watching.\n1318\t\n1319\tEvery component in this chapter reads from and writes to the same shared state, and that state is made of *objects*, the descriptions you submit and the system maintains. §6 kept saying \"the field that says what should be true\" and cross-bearing forward, because that field has a name, and a counterpart that reports what actually is true, and a set of conventions for how you write both. Chapter 4 gives them to you.\n1320\t\n1321\tThat's when the control loop stops being an architecture diagram and becomes something you operate. You'll write a description, submit it, and watch a loop you now understand go to work on it. The reason that will feel unremarkable rather than magical is that you did the work in this chapter first.\n1322\t\n1323\tNow that you look at a Kubernetes cluster and see loops rather than services, the rest of the book gets considerably easier.\n1324\t\n1325\t> *\"The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan, but because a few dozen small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing.\"*\n1326\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb38ee4c-6392-430d-ba14-888fa6adb2b7","uuid":"fe4a1f32-3920-4588-963a-cc5426d80306","timestamp":"2026-08-24T08:23:29.393Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md","content":"## The Voyage Ahead\n\nYou've met the crew and you know how they coordinate. What you don't yet have is the thing they're all watching.\n\nEvery component in this chapter reads from and writes to the same shared state, and that state is made of *objects*, the descriptions you submit and the system maintains. §6 kept saying \"the field that says what should be true\" and cross-bearing forward, because that field has a name, and a counterpart that reports what actually is true, and a set of conventions for how you write both. Chapter 4 gives them to you.\n\nThat's when the control loop stops being an architecture diagram and becomes something you operate. You'll write a description, submit it, and watch a loop you now understand go to work on it. The reason that will feel unremarkable rather than magical is that you did the work in this chapter first.\n\nNow that you look at a Kubernetes cluster and see loops rather than services, the rest of the book gets considerably easier.\n\n> *\"The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan, but because a few dozen small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing.\"*\n","numLines":12,"startLine":1315,"totalLines":1326}}}
