{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018kUGQDqijSUYZAf9t4nVoU","type":"tool_result","content":"2:source_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/\"\n6:concepts_covered: [\"kubectl\", \"kubeconfig\", \"kubectl-syntax\", \"get\", \"describe\", \"apply\", \"create\", \"delete\", \"logs\", \"exec\", \"scale\", \"rollout\", \"explain\"]\n8:# Command line tool (kubectl) (kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/)\n10:Kubernetes provides a command line tool for communicating with a Kubernetes cluster's control plane, using the Kubernetes API. For configuration, kubectl looks for a file named config in the $HOME/.kube directory. You can specify other kubeconfig files by setting the KUBECONFIG environment variable or by setting the --kubeconfig flag.\n13:[Omitted long matching line]\n16:By default kubectl will first determine if it is running within a pod, and thus in a cluster. It starts by checking for the KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST and KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT environment variables and the existence of a service account token file at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token. If all three are found in-cluster authentication is assumed. When kubectl runs in a cluster it acts against the namespace of the ServiceAccount unless --namespace is given.\n21:- apply — Apply a configuration change to a resource from a file or stdin."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb38ee4c-6392-430d-ba14-888fa6adb2b7","uuid":"7f0b4a06-d0c0-43d7-8ecd-04dac8071f89","timestamp":"2026-08-24T08:23:51.551Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"2:source_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/\"\n6:concepts_covered: [\"kubectl\", \"kubeconfig\", \"kubectl-syntax\", \"get\", \"describe\", \"apply\", \"create\", \"delete\", \"logs\", \"exec\", \"scale\", \"rollout\", \"explain\"]\n8:# Command line tool (kubectl) (kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/)\n10:Kubernetes provides a command line tool for communicating with a Kubernetes cluster's control plane, using the Kubernetes API. For configuration, kubectl looks for a file named config in the $HOME/.kube directory. You can specify other kubeconfig files by setting the KUBECONFIG environment variable or by setting the --kubeconfig flag.\n13:[Omitted long matching line]\n16:By default kubectl will first determine if it is running within a pod, and thus in a cluster. It starts by checking for the KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST and KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT environment variables and the existence of a service account token file at /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token. If all three are found in-cluster authentication is assumed. When kubectl runs in a cluster it acts against the namespace of the ServiceAccount unless --namespace is given.\n21:- apply — Apply a configuration change to a resource from a file or stdin.","numLines":7,"totalLines":7}}
